  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 322 AM CDT Mon Oct 12 2026\n\nPRECIPITATION CHANCES: \n\nLATE THIS AFTERNOON-EVENING...A slow-moving strong cold front will \ninch closer to the region from the north. A few of the CAMs initiate \nisolated thunderstorms in the vicinity of this front generally along \nand north of I-70 later today and into the evening. However, \nthinking this is overdone given NIL large-scale forcing and rather \nstout capping, so kept the forecast dry for now.\n\nTUESDAY AFTERNOON-NIGHT...Thinking this period has the greatest \npotential for at least a handful of strong to severe thunderstorms, \nas the slow-moving strong cold front moving in from the north \ninteracts with an unseasonably moist and unstable airmass, amidst 40-\n50 kts of deep layer effective shear. Lapse rates aren't amazing, so \nlikely not looking at giant hail, but possibly up to ping pong or \ngolfball size if a supercell or two can sustain itself, especially \nbefore 8-9 PM. Thereafter, the surging cold front and merging cold \npools should transition the surface-based thunderstorm threat to \nmainly 50-65 mph winds and marginal hail. Wouldn't be surprised to \nsee additional shower/thunderstorm development north of the surface \nfront as the evening and night progress, with a continued threat for \nmarginally severe hail given ample elevated instability and \neffective shear. Furthermore, Locally heavy rainfall is likely given \nthe unseasonably rich precipitable waters, and the potential for \ntraining showers/storms due to the parallel orientation of the front \nand mid-level flow.\n\nWEDNESDAY-THURSDAY...This period will feature off-and-on scattered \nto numerous showers/thunderstorms across the region, as a deep upper \ntrough approaches slowly from the west, and the deep cold frontal \nzone stalls across the area. Continued unseasonably rich \nprecipitable waters and strong forcing will lead to locally heavy \nrainfall and possibly some localized flooding issues. Limited \nbuoyancy should temper severe weather potential, although the strong \nforcing, strong flow aloft, and marginal buoyancy may support a few \nstrong to marginally severe thunderstorms.\n\nTEMPERATURES & WIND:\n\nTODAY-TUESDAY...The next few days will feature the warmest days of \nthe next 7-10, with unseasonable warmth in the upper 80s to low 90s. \nMay flirt with record highs both days. A tight pressure gradient \nwill support stout southerly winds today, with gusts upwards of 30-\n35 mph likely. \n\nWEDNESDAY-THURSDAY...Had to undercut NBM high temperatures, due\nto widespread clouds/precipitation along with the potential for\na further south placement of the cold front, and increasing \ncold advection by Thursday afternoon. Highs in the 60s-70s are \nlikely, warmest over southeast KS.\n\nFRIDAY-SATURDAY...A deepening central to eastern North American \nupper trough will drive strong Canadian high pressure south into Mid-\nAmerica, supporting the coolest air thus far this fall season. Went \nupper 50s to mid 60s for highs Friday and Saturday, although this \nmay still be a bit too warm. Additionally, stout/gusty north winds \nFriday will make it feel even cooler. The coolest night will be \nFriday night, with lows likely dipping into the 30s to low 40s \nunderneath the strong high pressure. Given the light winds and clear \nskies, later shifts may need to contemplate some patchy frost over \nmainly central KS.\n\nSUNDAY AND BEYOND...Model consensus supports a quick return to \nseasonable or even above normal temperatures, especially by next \nweek, as another deep upper trough sets up over the western CONUS.",
